#: manual/design/checked.md:1
msgid "checked design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:3
msgid "This page explains which operations on dual numbers have checked forms, how their errors arise from the derivative rules, and why the facade reuses the `arithmetic` error model."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:9
msgid "Let differentiated code report invalid operations as values, with the same error type that scalar Luna Flow code uses, and never return a valid value with an invalid derivative. The section on [floating-point domains](#floating-point-domains) shows where the current implementation falls short of the second goal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:17
msgid "The checked traits and errors belong to `Luna-Flow/arithmetic`; this repository can implement them for `Dual[T]` but cannot add variants."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:19
msgid "A checked dual operation can only use the checked operations of `T`, so its errors are those of `T`'s instances."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:21
msgid "`Dual[T]` keeps the unchecked operators `/` and `sqrt` for ordinary formulas; the checked forms are separate methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:26
msgid "Where a derivative fails"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:28
msgid "A checked operation must fail when either component of $f(a + b\\varepsilon) = f(a) + f'(a)\\,b\\,\\varepsilon$ is undefined. For the two checked operations:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:41
msgid ""
"The quotient fails exactly where the value fails, because $c^2 \\n"
"e 0 \\iff c \\n"
"e 0$ in exact arithmetic. The square root is different: $\\sqrt a$ exists at $a = 0$, but $\\sqrt{\\cdot}$ is not differentiable there (its difference quotient $\\sqrt h / h = h^{-1/2}$ is unbounded), so the domain of the dual operation is the open half-line $a > 0$, smaller than the domain $a \\ge 0$ of the scalar square root."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:48
msgid "Floating-point domains"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:50
msgid ""
"In `Double`, $c^2$ can be zero for non-zero $c$. With rounding to nearest and gradual underflow, $c^2$ rounds to $0$ exactly when $c^2 \\le 2^{-1075}$, half the smallest subnormal number, that is for $|c| < 2^{-537.5} \\approx 1.57 \\times 10^{-162}$, even though $a/c$ may be finite. The checked quotient then fails in the tangent: with a division by zero when $bc - ad \\n"
"e 0$, and with the domain error of $0/0$ when $bc - ad = 0$, for example for a constant divided by a constant, whose true tangent is $0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:59
msgid "At the other end $c^2$ overflows to $\\infty$ for $|c| \\ge 2^{512} \\approx 1.34 \\times 10^{154}$. `T`'s `div_checked` accepts a finite numerator over $\\infty$, so the checked quotient returns `Ok` with tangent $0$ even when the true tangent $(bc - ad)/c^2$ is a normal number. This breaks the design goal: a checked operation returns a wrong derivative without an error. Rescale such divisors; the [dual design](dual.md#rounding-error) derives the ranges and a formula that avoids squaring $c$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:70
msgid "Check both components"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:72
msgid "**Problem.** A checked scalar operation can succeed while the derivative does not exist."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:75
msgid "**Choice.** Each checked dual operation calls the checked scalar operation for the value and `div_checked` for the tangent division, and returns the first error. This makes the dual domain the intersection of the domains of $f$ and $f'$, as derived above; in particular `sqrt_checked` fails at $0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:80
msgid "No special case for a zero tangent"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:82
msgid "At $a = 0$ with $b = 0$ the tangent is the indeterminate $0/0$. One could return $0$ (a constant input has no derivative to report), but that would make the result depend on how a constant was produced. The implementation lets `T` decide: for `Double` this is a domain error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:87
msgid "Reuse `arithmetic`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:89
msgid "`Dual[T]` implements `DivChecked` and `SqrtChecked` from `arithmetic`, and this facade re-exports exactly those traits with `ArithmeticContext`, `ArithmeticError`, `ArithmeticErrorKind` and `RoundingMode`. Generic checked code therefore runs on `Double` and on `Dual[Double]` without adaptation, and errors from both levels have one type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:95
msgid "Only division and square root"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:97
msgid "`arithmetic` defines checked traits for division, square root, comparison, parsing and integer powers. Of these, only division and square root are operations on $T[\\varepsilon]$ with a derivative rule in this repository, so only they have checked dual forms. Logarithms and trigonometric functions follow the unchecked semantics of `T`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:105
msgid "`div_checked(x, y)` succeeds exactly when `T`'s `div_checked` succeeds for both $a/c$ and $(bc - ad)/c^2$; then it equals `x / y` computed with the same operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:108
msgid "`sqrt_checked(x)` succeeds exactly when `T`'s `sqrt_checked(a)` and `div_checked(b, 2√a)` succeed; for `Double` that is $a > 0$, or $a$ NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:110
msgid "The context is passed to `T` unchanged and never modified."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:114
msgid "**A dedicated autodiff error type.** It would duplicate `ArithmeticError` and force conversions at every boundary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:116
msgid "**`Option` results.** They lose the reason for the failure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:117
msgid "**Checking only the value.** It would return infinite or NaN derivatives from a \"checked\" operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:122
msgid "No checked logarithm, exponential or trigonometric operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:123
msgid "No contextual (`ArithmeticOutcome`) operations and no rounding diagnostics."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/checked.md:125
msgid "No automatic rescaling of tiny divisors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:1
msgid "core design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:3
msgid "This page explains why the `core` facade exists and why it contains exactly the ring-level vocabulary of `Dual[T]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:8
msgid "Offer the smallest import for code that only needs the algebra of dual numbers: ring operations, identities and integer constants, without the analytic traits and error types of `arithmetic`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:14
msgid "A facade can only re-export names; it cannot add instances or methods, which live with `Dual[T]` in `dual`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:16
msgid "The name `core` is taken inside this module, so examples import the package under the alias `@ad_core`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:21
msgid "Many differentiable programs are polynomial: they use only $+$, $-$, $\\times$ and integer constants. For those, the identity of the [dual design](dual.md#polynomials-where-derivatives-come-from)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:29
msgid "holds in every commutative ring, so the traits `Zero`, `One`, `AddMonoid`, `AddGroup`, `MulMonoid`, `Semiring`, `Ring` and the canonical map $\\mathbb Z \\to T$ (`FromInteger`, with its supertrait `FromNat`) are all such code needs. The facade re-exports exactly these. In `luna-generic` each trait extends the ones before it,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:44
msgid "so the classes of structures shrink as the traits grow: every ring is a semiring and an additive group, and every semiring and every additive group is an additive monoid,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:54
msgid "The instances on $T[\\varepsilon]$ are derived in the [dual design](dual.md#ring-level-instances-only)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:59
msgid "A separate facade for the algebra"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:61
msgid "**Problem.** The root package also re-exports the analytic traits and the checked error types, which belong to another layer of the ecosystem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:64
msgid "**Choice.** `core` re-exports only `Dual` and the `luna-generic` structure traits. Its `moon.pkg` imports `dual` and `luna-generic` only, so a reader of an import list can see that the code is purely algebraic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:68
msgid "Re-export, do not redefine"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:70
msgid "As in the [autodiff design](autodiff.md#re-export-with-pub-using), the names are `pub using` aliases of the original traits, so instances are shared with the rest of Luna Flow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:76
msgid "`core` defines no items; its interface file contains only `pub using` lines."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:78
msgid "It depends on `autodiff/dual` and `luna-generic` and on nothing else."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:79
msgid "Every re-exported trait has an instance on `Dual[T]` under the matching bound on `T`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:84
msgid "**Merging `core` into the root package.** The root package also carries `arithmetic`; keeping the algebra apart keeps that layering visible."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:86
msgid "**Re-exporting `Field` or `Inverse`.** `Dual[T]` does not implement them, so they would only invite unsatisfiable bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:91
msgid "No analytic traits, no checked operations, no drivers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:92
msgid "No `Field`, `MulGroup` or `Inverse`, and none of the `NatHomomorphism` / `IntegralHomomorphism` traits that `luna-generic` 0.4.0 deprecates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:1
msgid "dual design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:3
msgid "This page explains the mathematics of `Dual[T]` and why the type has exactly the operations and instances listed in the [dual API](../api/dual.md). The [dual tutorial](../tutorial/dual.md) uses the type without this background."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:10
msgid "Compute exact first derivatives of ordinary programs, written against the Luna Flow traits, by running the same program on a different number type. The type has to satisfy the algebraic laws its instances advertise, report domain failures through the shared `arithmetic` error values, and stay independent of any container or polynomial library."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:18
msgid "MoonBit has no operator overloading for mixed types, so a literal cannot be added to a `Dual[T]`; every constant enters through `Dual::constant` or a trait such as `FromInteger`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:21
msgid "Luna Flow traits are single-parameter traits on `Self`, so `Dual[T]` can only claim a structure for every `T` with a given bound, never for one particular `T`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:24
msgid "The tangent must live in the same type as the value: a generic `Dual[T]` cannot store a vector of tangents without a second type parameter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:27
msgid "The checked tier must use the error values of `Luna-Flow/arithmetic`, so that dual and scalar failures share one vocabulary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:32
msgid "The algebra of dual numbers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:34
msgid "Let $R$ be a commutative ring (for floating-point types, the real numbers they approximate). The dual numbers over $R$ are the quotient of the polynomial ring $R[x]$ by the ideal generated by $x^2$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:42
msgid "Every class has exactly one representative of degree at most one, so every element is $a + b\\varepsilon$ with unique $a, b \\in R$. This is the pair `(value, tangent)`. Because $R[\\varepsilon]$ is a quotient of a commutative ring by an ideal, it is itself a commutative ring, and its operations are those of polynomials followed by dropping $\\varepsilon^2$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:57
msgid "Units and division"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:59
msgid "$a + b\\varepsilon$ is invertible exactly when $a$ is. If $a$ is a unit, then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:66
msgid "Conversely, if $(a + b\\varepsilon)(c + d\\varepsilon) = 1$ then $ac = 1$, so $a$ is a unit. In particular $\\varepsilon$ is a non-zero element without an inverse, and $\\varepsilon \\cdot \\varepsilon = 0$ makes it a zero divisor: $R[\\varepsilon]$ is never a field, even when $R$ is. For a unit $c$ the quotient is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:78
msgid "which is the formula `Dual::div` and `Dual::div_checked` implement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:80
msgid "Polynomials: where derivatives come from"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:82
msgid "For $n \\ge 1$ the binomial theorem and $\\varepsilon^2 = 0$ give"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:89
msgid "By linearity, every polynomial $p(x) = \\sum_k c_k x^k$ with coefficients in $R$ satisfies"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:97
msgid "where $p'$ is the formal derivative. Nothing here needs limits: the identity holds in every commutative ring, including the integers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:100
msgid "Smooth functions and the chain rule"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:102
msgid "For a differentiable function $f$ that is not a polynomial, the package defines the extension to dual numbers by the same identity,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:109
msgid "which is the first-order Taylor expansion $f(a + h) = f(a) + f'(a)h + O(h^2)$ with the infinitesimal $h = b\\varepsilon$; the remainder vanishes because $\\varepsilon^2 = 0$. Composition then produces the chain rule without any extra code. For differentiable $g$ and $f$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:123
msgid "The sum, product and quotient rules are the ring operations above read with $b = u'(x)$ and $d = v'(x)$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:131
msgid "The elementary rules of the package are this definition applied to known derivatives:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:134
msgid "Method"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:136
msgid "$b / (2r)$ with $r = \\sqrt a$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:137
msgid "$b \\cdot v$ with $v = e^a$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:138
msgid "$b \\cdot v \\cdot \\ln 2$ with $v = 2^a$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:146
msgid "The value projection is a homomorphism"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:148
msgid "The map $\\pi : R[\\varepsilon] \\to R$, $a + b\\varepsilon \\mapsto a$, preserves $0$, $1$, $+$, $-$ and $\\times$: $\\pi\\big((a + b\\varepsilon)(c + d\\varepsilon)\\big) = ac = \\pi(a + b\\varepsilon)\\,\\pi(c + d\\varepsilon)$, and likewise for the others. Every elementary rule also has $\\pi(f(z)) = f(\\pi(z))$ by definition. Hence the `value` of any computation on dual numbers is exactly the result of the same computation on the values: adding tangents never changes the primal result, not even its rounding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:158
msgid "One generic type over the scalar"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:160
msgid "**Problem.** Derivatives are needed for `Double`, `Float`, and for scalar types defined in other Luna Flow packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:163
msgid "**Options.** A `Double`-only dual type; a generic `Dual[T]` whose operations ask for the smallest trait set they use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:166
msgid "**Choice.** `Dual[T]` is generic, and every method carries its own bound (`Dual::mul` needs only `Add + Mul`, `Dual::exp2` needs `Exponential + Logarithmic + FromInteger + Mul`). This follows the Luna Flow rule of depending on the smallest trait composition. It also lets `T` itself be a dual number, which gives higher derivatives by nesting (see the [forward design](forward.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:173
msgid "Ring-level instances only"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:175
msgid "**Problem.** Which structure traits from `luna-generic` may `Dual[T]` implement?"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:178
msgid "**Choice.** `Zero`, `One`, `AddMonoid`, `AddGroup`, `MulMonoid`, `Semiring` and `Ring`, each when `T` has the same structure. These are equational classes: their laws are identities between terms, and identities are inherited by the quotient $T[x]/(x^2)$. As a check, associativity of the product rule:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:193
msgid ""
"`Field`, `MulGroup` and `Inverse` are rejected because the [units computation](#units-and-division) shows that $\\varepsilon \\n"
"e 0$ has no inverse: an `Inverse` instance would have to return a wrong value for it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:197
msgid "No ordering"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:199
msgid "**Problem.** Generic code often branches on `<`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:201
msgid "**Options.** Order by `value` only; order lexicographically by `(value, tangent)`; no order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:204
msgid "**Choice.** No `Compare` instance. Ordering by value is not antisymmetric with respect to the derived `Eq` ($a + b\\varepsilon$ and $a + c\\varepsilon$ would each be $\\le$ the other without being equal). The lexicographic order is total but is not a ring order: it makes $\\varepsilon > 0$, and an ordered ring requires $x > 0, y > 0 \\Rightarrow xy > 0$, while $\\varepsilon \\cdot \\varepsilon = 0$. Code that branches compares `x.value()` explicitly, which also makes visible that the derivative is the derivative of the branch taken."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:212
msgid "An unchecked `Div` despite not being a field"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:214
msgid "**Problem.** Ordinary formulas use `/`, and MoonBit's `/` operator needs the `Div` trait; but division is partial on $R[\\varepsilon]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:217
msgid "**Choice.** `Dual[T]` implements `Div` with the quotient formula and no check, inheriting `T`'s behaviour on a zero divisor (for `Double`, infinities or NaN). This mirrors the unchecked tier of `arithmetic`, where `Sqrt` and friends follow the IEEE semantics of the scalar. The checked tier is `DivChecked`, implemented on top of `T`'s own `DivChecked`. `Div` alone is not a structure claim; the type still does not implement `Field`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:224
msgid "Checked forms reuse `arithmetic`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:226
msgid "**Problem.** Division by zero and square roots of negative numbers must be reportable as data."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:229
msgid "**Options.** A dedicated autodiff error type; the `arithmetic` traits and error values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:232
msgid "**Choice.** `Dual[T]` implements `DivChecked` and `SqrtChecked` from `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` and returns its `ArithmeticError`, so callers handle dual and scalar failures with one vocabulary. Both the value and the tangent computation are checked, and the first failure is returned. Only division and square root have checked forms, because those are the checked traits `arithmetic` defines; logarithms and trigonometric functions follow the unchecked semantics of `T`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:240
msgid "Constants through the integers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:242
msgid "**Problem.** The rules for `sqrt`, `exp2`, `log2` and `log10` need the constants $2$ and $10$ in `T`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:245
msgid "**Options.** Require a conversion from `Double`; build them as `one + one`; use the canonical map from the integers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:248
msgid "**Choice.** `lift_to(2)` and `lift_to(10)` from `luna-generic`, which pass the `Int` through `BigInt` to `FromInteger::from_integer`. The map $\\mathbb Z \\to T$ is the unique ring homomorphism, so it names the right constant in every ring, and small integers such as $2$ and $10$ are exact in every numeric instance. A `Double` conversion would not exist for exact types, and repeated addition costs more for $10$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:255
msgid "Reuse the computed value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:257
msgid "`exp` and `exp2` compute $v = f(a)$ once and form the tangent from $v$, because $f' = f$ (up to the factor $\\ln 2$). `sqrt` likewise divides by the computed root. This saves one elementary evaluation per call and makes the tangent consistent with the returned value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:262
msgid "The derivative of `tan`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:264
msgid "Two formulas give $\\tan' a$: $1 + \\tan^2 a$, which reuses the value, and $\\sec^2 a = 1/\\cos^2 a$, which needs one more cosine. The package uses $b / (\\cos a \\cdot \\cos a)$. Both are mathematically equal; the chosen form is undefined at exactly the same points as $\\tan$ itself."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:269
msgid "Explicit method promotion"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:271
msgid "With MoonBit 0.10, trait instances no longer create methods implicitly. [`src/dual/extends.mbt`](../../../src/dual/extends.mbt) promotes the arithmetic operators, `equal`, `zero`, `one`, `from_natural`, `from_integer` and `div_checked`, and keeps `not_equal`, `to_repr`, `from_nat`, `from_integral`, `pi`, `e` and `tau` as hidden deprecated forms for existing callers (see [Deprecated](../api/dual.md#deprecated))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:278
msgid "Compatibility shims for the deprecated homomorphism traits"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:280
msgid "`luna-generic` 0.4.0 deprecates `NatHomomorphism` and `IntegralHomomorphism`: they compose a lift to ℕ or ℤ with the canonical map, which is not a homomorphism for fixed-width sources. `Dual[T]` now implements `FromNat` and `FromInteger`. The old instances stay in [`src/dual/compat.mbt`](../../../src/dual/compat.mbt), because `luna-poly` 0.2.0 bounds `DensePolynomial::derivative` by `NatHomomorphism` and would otherwise reject dual coefficients. Each shim suppresses the deprecation warning locally, and all of them go once `luna-poly` moves to `FromNat`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:291
msgid "Derivatives of whole programs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:293
msgid "Let a program compute $y = f(x)$ by a finite sequence of steps, each a ring operation, a division or one of the elementary functions of the table. Run it on $x + 1\\varepsilon$ (`Dual::variable(x)`) with every other input a constant. Then every intermediate $v_k$ is represented as $v_k(x) + v_k'(x)\\,\\varepsilon$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:299
msgid ""
"*Proof by induction on the steps.* Inputs: the variable is $x + 1\\varepsilon$ and $x' = 1$; a constant is $c + 0\\varepsilon$ and $c' = 0$. Step: let the operands be $u + u'\\varepsilon$ and $w + w'\\varepsilon$, where $u$ and $w$ are functions of $x$ differentiable at the point. For a binary operation $\\star \\in \\{+, -, \\times, /\\}$ (with $w(x) \\n"
"e 0$ for $/$), the formulas derived above give"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:310
msgid "because they are exactly the sum, difference, product and quotient rules. An elementary function $g$, applied in the open set where it is differentiable, gives $g(u) + g'(u)\\,u'\\,\\varepsilon = g(u) + (g \\circ u)'\\varepsilon$ by the chain rule. The last intermediate is $y$, so `tangent` is $f'(x)$. $\\square$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:316
msgid ""
"The same argument with input tangent $b$ gives $f'(x)\\,b$, and with several inputs seeded by a vector $v$ it gives the directional derivative $\\n"
"abla f(x) \\cdot v$ (see the [linalg design](linalg.md)). The derivative is that of the program, not of a mathematical function it approximates: a branch on `value()` differentiates the branch taken."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:322
msgid "Rounding error"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:324
msgid "Write $\\mathrm{fl}(x \\circ y) = (x \\circ y)(1 + \\delta)$ with $|\\delta| \\le u$ for each floating-point operation ($u = 2^{-53}$ for `Double`), and use the standard lemma that a product of $k$ factors $(1 + \\delta_i)^{\\pm 1}$ equals $1 + \\theta_k$ with $|\\theta_k| \\le \\gamma_k = ku/(1 - ku)$.[^higham]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:329
msgid "N. J. Higham, *Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms*, 2nd ed., SIAM, 2002, Lemma 3.1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:332
msgid "*Product tangent.* `Dual::mul` computes $\\mathrm{fl}(\\mathrm{fl}(ad) + \\mathrm{fl}(cb))$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:343
msgid "*Quotient tangent.* `Dual::div` computes $\\mathrm{fl}\\big(\\mathrm{fl}(bc - ad) / \\mathrm{fl}(c \\cdot c)\\big)$, five roundings with one of them in the denominator:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:355
msgid "Both bounds have the shape of the error of evaluating the derivative formula directly; the relative error is only large when $ad$ and $bc$ cancel, which is the conditioning of the derivative itself. For an elementary rule the tangent error is the error of `T`'s implementation of $f'(a)$ (for example of `cos` in `sin`) plus at most two more roundings."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:361
msgid "The model $\\mathrm{fl}(x \\circ y) = (x \\circ y)(1 + \\delta)$ only holds while no result leaves the normal range of `T`. For `Double` that range is $[2^{-1022}, 2^{1024})$ in magnitude, and the quotient tangent leaves it much earlier than the quotient, because it squares the divisor:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:374
msgid "The first line holds because a square below half the smallest subnormal, $2^{-1075}$, rounds to zero. In the first case the tangent is a division by zero, in the second it carries a relative error up to $2^{-1075}/c^2$ instead of $u$, and in the third it is $0$ whenever $bc - ad$ is finite, although the true tangent $(bc - ad)/c^2$ can be a normal number: for $a = 0$, $b = 1$, $c = 10^{155}$ it is $10^{-155}$. Writing the tangent as $(b - q\\,d)/c$ with $q = a/c$, which is the same expression in exact arithmetic because $(bc - ad)/c^2 = (b - (a/c)\\,d)/c$, would avoid squaring $c$; the current code does not do this (see the warning on `Dual::div_checked`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:385
msgid "Comparison with finite differences"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:387
msgid "The forward difference $D_h f(x) = (f(x + h) - f(x))/h$ has two error sources. Taylor's theorem gives the truncation error, and evaluating $f$ in floating point with error at most $u|f|$ adds a cancellation error:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:395
msgid "Setting the derivative of the right-hand side with respect to $h$ to zero,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:404
msgid "so even the best step loses about half of the significant digits ($\\sqrt u \\approx 10^{-8}$ for `Double`). The central difference $(f(x + h) - f(x - h))/(2h)$ has truncation error $\\frac{h^2}{6}|f'''|$ and reaches about $u^{2/3} \\approx 10^{-11}$. Dual numbers have no truncation term at all: by the induction above they compute the exact derivative of the program, and the only error is rounding with the bounds of the previous section.[^griewank] The test suite and the [dual tutorial](../tutorial/dual.md) compare both methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:413
msgid "A. Griewank and A. Walther, *Evaluating Derivatives*, 2nd ed., SIAM, 2008, chapters 2–3, treat forward mode and its error analysis in full."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:416
#: manual/design/poly.md:162
msgid "Cost"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:418
msgid "Addition, subtraction and negation cost two operations of `T` instead of one; multiplication costs three multiplications and one addition; division costs three multiplications, one subtraction and two divisions; an elementary function costs the function plus its derivative factor (for `sin`, one `sin`, one `cos` and one multiplication). Counted in operations of `T`, the ratio is $2$ for linear operations, $4$ for a product, $6$ for a quotient and about $2$ for an elementary function, so a program runs a small constant factor, at most about six, slower on `Dual[T]` than on `T`, and needs twice the memory for its numbers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:428
msgid "Laws to keep"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:430
msgid "`x.value()` of any result equals the same computation on values (the projection is a homomorphism)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:432
msgid "Constants, `zero()`, `one()`, `from_natural`, `from_integer` (and the deprecated `from_nat`, `from_integral`) and the `Constants` instance have tangent zero."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:435
msgid "Instances stop at `Ring`; no `Field`, `MulGroup`, `Inverse` or `Compare`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:436
msgid "The product rule assumes commutative multiplication of `T`, which every numeric instance in Luna Flow has."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:441
msgid "**Finite differences.** Simple, but they lose half the digits or more, as derived above, and need a step size per problem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:443
msgid "**Symbolic differentiation.** Building derivative expressions needs a term language and simplification, and expressions can grow quickly; that belongs to a CAS layer, not to a scalar type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:446
msgid "**Reverse mode.** Better for gradients of many inputs, but it needs a tape or closures that record the computation; it is not implemented."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:448
msgid "**Storing a vector of tangents.** One pass would give a whole gradient, at the cost of a vector-valued type and allocation on every operation. The scalar tangent keeps `Dual[T]` a plain two-field value; vector tangents are future work."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:455
msgid "First derivatives only. Higher derivatives come from nesting `Dual[Dual[T]]`; the $k$-th derivative stores $2^k$ components and a product costs $3^k$ multiplications and $3^k - 2^k$ additions of `T` (see the [forward design](forward.md#higher-derivatives-by-nesting)). There is no truncated Taylor (jet) type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:460
msgid "No reverse mode and no symbolic differentiation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:461
msgid "No `Field`, `MulGroup`, `Inverse` or order instance, and no `Show`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:462
msgid "Only `div_checked` and `sqrt_checked` check their domain. Logarithms, trigonometric functions and the unchecked operators follow `T`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/dual.md:464
msgid "No validated enclosures: the rounding bounds above are a priori, not computed at run time. Interval or ball scalars as `T` are neither tested nor documented by this repository."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:1
msgid "elementary design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:3
msgid "This page explains the `elementary` facade: which analytic traits it exposes, how their derivative rules are justified, and where they stop."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:8
msgid "Make analytic code written against the `arithmetic` traits differentiable without change, by exposing exactly the traits that `Dual[T]` implements with a correct derivative rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:14
msgid "A trait instance must implement every method of the trait, so a trait appears here only when `Dual[T]` has a rule for all of its methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:16
msgid "The traits belong to `Luna-Flow/arithmetic`; this repository adds instances, never traits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:21
msgid "Every rule has the form $f(a + b\\varepsilon) = f(a) + f'(a)\\,b\\,\\varepsilon$ (the [dual design](dual.md#smooth-functions-and-the-chain-rule) derives the chain rule from it). The derivatives used are the classical ones; for the inverse-function and base-change cases they follow from the chain rule:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:38
msgid ""
"Each formula holds on the open domain where $f$ is differentiable: $a > 0$ for the square root and the logarithms, $\\cos a \\n"
"e 0$ for the tangent, and everywhere for $\\exp$, $\\sin$ and $\\cos$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:44
msgid "Expose what `Dual[T]` implements"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:46
msgid "The facade re-exports `Sqrt`, `SqrtChecked`, `Exponential`, `Logarithmic`, `Trigonometric` and `Constants`, the analytic traits with an instance on `Dual[T]`. `arithmetic` also defines `Hyperbolic`, `InverseTrigonometric`, `InverseHyperbolic`, `Cbrt` and `Power`; `Dual[T]` has no instance for them yet, so they are not re-exported."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:52
msgid "Instances need the whole trait"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:54
msgid "A trait instance must provide all its methods. `Exponential` contains `exp2`, whose rule needs $\\ln 2$, so the instance needs `Logarithmic` and `FromInteger` on `T` even if a caller only uses `exp`. The inherent method `Dual::exp` has the smaller bound `Exponential + Mul` for that case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:59
msgid "Constants have tangent zero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:61
msgid "$\\pi$, $\\tau$ and $e$ do not depend on the differentiation variable, so the `Constants` instance embeds them with `Dual::constant`. This is the structure-preserving choice: the map $T \\to T[\\varepsilon]$, $c \\mapsto c + 0\\varepsilon$, is a ring homomorphism."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:68
msgid "Each rule matches the derivative table above and is tested on `Double` (`sin`, `exp` and others in `src/tests/dual_test.mbt`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:70
msgid "Outside the open domain, each component follows `T` separately: for `Double`, `ln(0)` is $-\\infty$ with tangent $b/0$, `sqrt` at $0$ has an infinite or NaN tangent, and `ln` at $a < 0$ has a NaN value with the finite tangent $b/a$, the derivative of $\\ln\\lvert a \\rvert$. A NaN in the value is therefore the reliable sign of a domain violation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:75
msgid "The tangent error is the error of `T`'s implementation of $f'(a)$ plus at most two roundings; see the [dual design](dual.md#rounding-error)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:80
msgid "**Re-exporting every analytic trait.** Bounds such as `T : Hyperbolic` would type-check for `T` but fail to instantiate at `Dual[T]`, which is confusing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:83
msgid "**Checked logarithms in this repository.** `arithmetic` has no checked logarithm trait to implement; adding one locally would fork the error model."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:89
msgid "No hyperbolic, inverse trigonometric, inverse hyperbolic, cube-root or general power rules."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:91
msgid "No checked forms other than `SqrtChecked`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/elementary.md:92
msgid "No branch-cut or complex-domain semantics; see [luna-complex](https://lunaflow.cn/en/luna-complex/) for complex functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:1
msgid "forward design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:3
msgid "This page explains what the scalar drivers `diff` and `value_and_diff` compute, what they cost, and why they are plain functions over `Dual[T] -> Dual[T]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:9
msgid "Give the common case, the derivative of a function of one variable, a one-line API that hides the seeding of the variable, while keeping all semantics in `Dual[T]` so that the drivers cannot disagree with the type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:15
msgid "A MoonBit closure has one concrete type, so a driver can only take a function that is already written for `Dual[T]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:17
msgid "The drivers must not depend on containers, so that scalar users do not pull in `linear-algebra` or `luna-poly`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:22
msgid "Forward mode"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:24
msgid "A program computes $y = f(x)$ through intermediates $v_1, \\dots, v_m$, each an operation on earlier ones. Forward mode carries, next to every $v_k$, its derivative $\\dot v_k = dv_k/dx$, and updates it with the chain rule in the same order as the program:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:37
msgid "Dual arithmetic performs exactly this update: $v_k + \\dot v_k\\varepsilon$ is `Dual::new(v_k, dv_k)`, and the rules of the [dual design](dual.md#smooth-functions-and-the-chain-rule) are the partial derivatives of each $\\varphi_k$. Seeding $\\dot x = 1$ is `Dual::variable(x)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:43
msgid "Higher derivatives by nesting"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:45
msgid ""
"If `f` is generic in its scalar, it can be applied to `Dual[Dual[T]]`. Write $\\varepsilon_1$ for the outer perturbation (the tangent of `Dual[T]`) and $\\varepsilon_2$ for the inner one (the tangent of `Dual[Dual[T]]`), with $\\varepsilon_1^2 = \\varepsilon_2^2 = 0$ and $\\varepsilon_1\\varepsilon_2 = \\varepsilon_2\\varepsilon_1 \\n"
"e 0$. The outer `diff` seeds $x + \\varepsilon_1$; the inner `diff` seeds that number as its variable, so `f` runs on $x + \\varepsilon_1 + \\varepsilon_2$. Applying the first-order identity twice,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:63
msgid "The inner `diff` returns the coefficient of $\\varepsilon_2$, which is $f'(x) + f''(x)\\,\\varepsilon_1$, and the outer `diff` returns its $\\varepsilon_1$ coefficient, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:71
msgid "The two perturbations live at different nesting levels, so they cannot be mixed up without an explicit conversion: a value of the outer level enters the inner computation only through `Dual::constant`, which gives it inner tangent zero. This rules out the classic *perturbation confusion* of untyped nested forward mode, where the inner derivative picks up the outer perturbation.[^siskind]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:78
msgid "Nesting is expensive. Level $k$ stores $2^k$ components of `T`. If a product at level $k$ costs $m_k$ multiplications and $p_k$ additions of `T`, then $m_k = 3m_{k-1}$ and $p_k = 3p_{k-1} + 2^{k-1}$, because a dual product makes three products and one addition one level down, and an addition at level $k - 1$ costs $2^{k-1}$ additions. With $m_0 = 1$ and $p_0 = 0$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:89
msgid "so the $k$-th derivative of a multiplication-heavy program costs about $2 \\cdot 3^k$ times a plain evaluation, and many of the components are duplicates (both first-order components hold $f'(x)$). A truncated Taylor series would need only $k + 1$ components and $O(k^2)$ work per product."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:94
msgid "J. M. Siskind and B. A. Pearlmutter, \"Perturbation confusion and referential transparency\", IFL 2005, describes the problem for untyped implementations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:100
msgid "Functions, not a trait or a wrapper type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:102
msgid "**Problem.** How should a user hand a function to the driver?"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:104
msgid "**Options.** A trait implemented by a user type; a function on `Double` plus a separate derivative; a closure over `Dual[T]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:107
msgid "**Choice.** A closure `(Dual[T]) -> Dual[T]`. MoonBit closures are cheap, the type makes it explicit that the function must be written for dual numbers, and generic functions instantiate at `Dual[T]` without wrappers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:111
msgid "Minimal bound"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:113
msgid "`value_and_diff` needs only `T : One`, for the seed tangent. Everything else is required by the operations inside `f`, where the compiler checks it at the call site."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:117
msgid "Value from the same evaluation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:119
msgid "The value is read from the same dual evaluation as the derivative. By the [projection homomorphism](dual.md#the-value-projection-is-a-homomorphism) it is bit-for-bit the value `f` would compute on `T`, so no second evaluation is needed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:124
msgid "Location in its own package"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:126
msgid "The drivers live in `forward` so that the `dual` package stays a pure number type, and the root package re-exports them. `forward` depends only on `dual` and `luna-generic`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:132
msgid "`value_and_diff(f, x) == (f(variable(x)).value(), f(variable(x)).tangent())` and `diff(f, x)` is its second component."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:134
msgid "For a program of ring operations, divisions with non-zero divisors and the elementary functions in their domains, the result is $(f(x), f'(x))$ computed with the rounding bounds of the [dual design](dual.md#rounding-error)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:138
msgid "The cost is one evaluation of `f` on `Dual[T]`, a small constant factor over one evaluation on `T`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:143
msgid "**Numerical differentiation inside the driver.** Rejected for the accuracy reasons derived in the [dual design](dual.md#comparison-with-finite-differences)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:146
msgid "**A `diff_n` for the $n$-th derivative.** Nesting already gives it for generic functions, and a truncated Taylor type would be the efficient solution; neither is a reason for a second API today."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:152
msgid "Scalar input only. Several inputs are handled by the [linalg](linalg.md) drivers or by seeding `Dual::new` by hand."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:154
msgid "No checked variant: `f` decides whether it uses checked operations, and a checked `f` returns its own `Result`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/forward.md:156
msgid "No higher-order driver and no reverse mode."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:1
msgid "linalg design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:3
msgid "This page explains how the `linalg` drivers obtain gradients and Jacobians from scalar dual numbers, what each one costs, and why the package is shaped as a thin bridge to `linear-algebra`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:9
msgid "Differentiate functions $f : T^n \\to T$ and $f : T^n \\to T^m$ written with the immutable vectors of `linear-algebra`, reusing `Dual[T]` unchanged, with a small API whose results follow the usual matrix conventions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:15
msgid "`Dual[T]` carries one scalar tangent, so one evaluation yields one directional derivative."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:17
msgid "`linear-algebra` has no shape-error type shared with this repository."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:18
msgid "`Dual[T]` is only a ring, so `f` may use only the ring operations of the vectors and matrices."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:23
msgid "Directional derivatives from one pass"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:25
msgid "Let $f : \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R^m$ be differentiable at $x$ and let $v \\in \\mathbb R^n$. Seed every input as $x_j + v_j\\varepsilon$. By the [dual design](dual.md#derivatives-of-whole-programs) each output becomes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:34
msgid "so one evaluation on dual numbers yields the Jacobian-vector product $J_f(x) v$. This is the multivariate chain rule: for $g(t) = f(x + tv)$, $g'(0) = J_f(x) v$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:38
msgid "Columns by unit seeds"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:40
msgid "Seeding the unit vector $e_j$ returns the $j$-th column of the Jacobian, $J_f(x) e_j = \\partial f / \\partial x_j$. The drivers make $n$ such passes:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:47
msgid ""
"For $m = 1$ the single row is $\\n"
"abla f(x)^{\\mathsf T}$, and `gradient` returns it as a vector."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:50
msgid "Cost compared with reverse mode"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:52
msgid "Let $C(f)$ be the cost of evaluating $f$ on $T$. One dual pass costs at most a small constant $c$ times $C(f)$, with $c \\le 6$ and typically $3$ to $4$ (see the [dual design](dual.md#cost)), so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:61
msgid "Reverse mode, which this repository does not implement, propagates sensitivities the other way. Write the program as intermediates $v_k = \\varphi_k(v_i)_{i \\prec k}$ ending in the outputs, and define the adjoint $\\bar v_i = \\sum_{\\ell} u_\\ell\\, \\partial y_\\ell / \\partial v_i$ for a chosen output weight $u \\in T^m$, so that $\\bar y = u$. Because $v_i$ influences the outputs only through the $v_k$ that use it, the chain rule gives the backward recurrence"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:74
msgid "evaluated from the outputs back to the inputs. One backward sweep therefore yields a whole row combination $u^{\\mathsf T} J_f(x)$, and for $m = 1$, $u = 1$, the whole gradient. Each elementary step has a bounded number of partial derivatives, so the sweep costs a constant multiple of $C(f)$, independent of $n$, at the price of storing the intermediates (or recomputing them).[^cheap] The two modes are transposes of each other: forward mode computes $J_f(x)\\,v$, reverse mode $u^{\\mathsf T} J_f(x)$. Forward mode is therefore the right tool when $n$ is small or $n \\lesssim m$, and the slower one for gradients of functions of many variables."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:85
msgid "This is the \"cheap gradient principle\"; see A. Griewank and A. Walther, *Evaluating Derivatives*, 2nd ed., SIAM, 2008, section 4.6."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:90
msgid "Scalar tangents and $n$ passes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:92
msgid "**Problem.** A gradient needs $n$ directional derivatives."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:94
msgid "**Options.** A dual type with a vector of $n$ tangents (one pass, $O(n)$ work per operation); $n$ passes with the scalar `Dual[T]`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:97
msgid "**Choice.** $n$ passes. The total arithmetic is the same order, $O(n \\cdot C(f))$, and the scalar type needs no allocation per operation and no new number type. A vector-tangent type remains future work."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:101
msgid "Output-by-input convention"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:103
msgid "The matrix is $m \\times n$ with entry $(i, j) = \\partial f_i / \\partial x_j$, the convention of the chain rule $J_{f \\circ g} = J_f\\,J_g$, so results compose with ordinary matrix multiplication. Column $j$ is pass $j$; the implementation collects the $n$ output vectors and then builds the matrix with `Matrix::make(m, n, (i, j) => columns[j][i].tangent())`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:109
msgid "A separate pass for values and for $m$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:111
msgid "`value_and_gradient` and `value_and_jacobian` evaluate `f` once more with all tangents zero instead of reading the value from one of the seeded passes. By the [projection homomorphism](dual.md#the-value-projection-is-a-homomorphism) every pass has the same value, so this costs one evaluation but keeps the drivers independent of each other and correct for $n = 0$. `jacobian` uses the same zero-tangent call to learn $m$ before allocating the matrix."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:118
msgid "No shape validation yet"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:120
msgid "**Problem.** A function may read past its input or change its output length."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:123
msgid "**Options.** Return `Result` with a shape error; abort; document a precondition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:126
msgid "**Choice.** A documented precondition. `linear-algebra` does not yet share a shape-error type with this repository, and a private error type would be replaced later. The source carries a `TODO` for checked variants."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:130
msgid "Only ring operations on `Dual[T]`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:132
msgid "The drivers need nothing but `One + Zero` for seeding. Vector and matrix operations inside `f` use the `Add`, `Mul` and `Neg` instances of `Dual[T]`, so any `linear-algebra` operation that only needs ring structure works on dual vectors. Algorithms that need `Field`, `Inverse` or an order on the scalar (pivoting, for example) cannot be instantiated at `Dual[T]`; this is intentional, see the [dual design](dual.md#ring-level-instances-only)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:141
msgid "`gradient(f, x)[j]` $= \\partial f / \\partial x_j (x)$ and `jacobian(f, x)[i][j]` $= \\partial f_i / \\partial x_j (x)$ for programs satisfying the [preconditions](../api/linalg.md#function-shapes-and-preconditions), with the rounding bounds of the dual design for each entry."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:145
msgid ""
"`value_and_gradient(f, x)` $= (f(x), \\n"
"abla f(x))$ and `value_and_jacobian(f, x)` $= (f(x), J_f(x))$; the values are exactly what `f` computes on `T`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:148
msgid "Evaluations: `gradient` $n$, `value_and_gradient` $n + 1$, `jacobian` $n + 1$, `value_and_jacobian` $n + 2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:150
msgid "Memory: `jacobian` holds the $n$ output vectors of length $m$ before building the $m \\times n$ matrix."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:155
msgid "**Reverse mode for gradients.** Asymptotically cheaper for large $n$, but it needs a recorded computation and is not implemented."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:157
msgid "**A public Jacobian-vector product.** One pass with `Dual::new(x[j], v[j])` already gives $J_f(x) v$ (see the [linalg tutorial](../tutorial/linalg.md#compute-a-jacobian-vector-product)); a dedicated function would add little."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:161
msgid "**Mutable matrices.** The drivers return `immut` values, which match the value semantics of the rest of the repository."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:166
msgid "Forward mode only: no reverse mode, and no Hessian or higher-order driver."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:168
msgid "No shape checking and no checked variants."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/linalg.md:169
msgid "Dense `immut` vectors and matrices only; no sparse or mutable containers."
